{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c55d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae2b-e184-7c10-8d9d-bac4c231b352"},"sequence":265,"recorded_at":1780531400000525,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0ae2c-8a46-7641-b741-f8df457129f5"},"task_id":"01a0ae2c-8a46-7641-b741-f8df457129f5","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0ae2c-8a46-7641-b741-f8df457129f5","chunk":"Read text file `internal/provhost/doc.go`.\n1|// Package provhost implements the ax host side of the provider plugin\n2|// JSON-over-stdio protocol of pinned specification Section 7.2: one-frame\n3|// JSONL transport, deadlines, size limits, stdout/stderr separation,\n4|// operation dispatch, and status recovery.\n5|//\n6|// The actor every Section 7.2 RFC 2119 keyword governs here is the ax host\n7|// (this repository). The provider plugin is a child process this package\n8|// starts but never trusts: every byte it emits is attacker-influenced, so\n9|// every frame is validated before any member is used, and a different-major\n10|// payload is never parsed far enough to trust its error code, retryable\n11|// bit, details, or authority fields (Section 15.1).\n12|//\n13|// This package owns the envelope layer and the operation-layer\n14|// conformance decoders. Operation dispatch means the host maps an\n15|// operation name to one plugin invocation and refuses unknown names\n16|// locally without spawning; the conformance decoders validate what a\n17|// plugin returned: the closed manifest (7.3), probe and capability\n18|// gate (7.4, 8.3), profile mapping (7.7), quiescence proof (7.6),\n19|// SpawnPlan success bodies for launch and resume, the Provider\n20|// Identity Record and identify-session result (5.5), and the single\n21|// (operation, operation_id) mutation key (7.5). Full Section 7.5\n22|// request-body vocabularies beyond these surfaces, and the\n23|// materialize commit/rollback result bodies, cross this package\n24|// opaquely: a body must still be a JSON object, and the\n25|// materialize-status body this package interprets for recovery must\n26|// satisfy the state/nullability/identity rules Section 7.5 states\n27|// for ProviderTransactionStatus.\n28|//\n29|// A failed read is never an absence: a crashed, timed-out, oversized,\n30|// unparsable, or correlation-mismatched invocation fails with a local\n31|// Structured Error 1.0.0, never with a partial response the caller could\n32|// mistake for a result. Status recovery is fail-closed the same way: an\n33|// unknown or integrity-invalid transaction fails with integrity_failure\n34|// and must be quarantined rather than represented as a successful status.\n35|//\n36|// The package mutates no durable state of its own and keeps no cross-call\n37|// cache: Host starts one plugin process per operation, so successive calls\n38|// are separate processes observing durable state through the authority the\n39|// caller passes, which is the property the cross-process recovery cases\n40|// rest on. Retry decisions belong to the caller; every local failure this\n41|// package emits is non-retryable, because nothing observed at an unusable\n42|// frame licenses a safe-retry claim.\n43|//\n44|// Stated bounds: stderr capture is capped at 1 MiB (an implementation\n45|// resource bound, not a protocol limit); error details carry the failure\n46|// class, member names, and the observed version string only — never\n47|// environment or stderr content. Mutation receipts live in the\n48|// provider transaction document and the plugin, not in the host: the\n49|// host holds no cross-call cache, so cross-crash durability of\n50|// (operation, operation_id) results is the document's property, and\n51|// the host side pins the sole key shape, byte-identical retry bytes,\n52|// and the mismatch surface. The Section 8 provider/platform cell\n53|// values are evidence labels, not host behavior: the host pins the\n54|// fail-closed direction (conditional, unsupported, and unknown are\n55|// never usable) for every capability-by-status tuple rather than one\n56|// cell value, resolves the Section 8.2 documented store roots for\n57|// discovery binding, and refuses the Section 8.4 disabled resume\n58|// tuples. Section 8.2 store exclusions and the remaining Section 7.5\n59|// request vocabularies have no host production yet.\n60|//\n61|// Provider Protocol 3.0.0 (§7.A), which the v0.5.0 catalog pins\n62|// alongside 2.0.0, is owned by internal/terminalbackend, not by this\n63|// package: the v3-specific production rule — parsing the closed v3\n64|// TerminalDescriptor and matching it against the AX-validated\n65|// host-local binding before a provider process is launched or observed\n66|// — is terminalbackend.AdmitProviderDescriptor, and the traceability\n67|// registry records section 7.A against terminalbackend for it. This\n68|// host speaks major 2 only: it classifies any recognizable 3.x\n69|// envelope as incompatible_protocol without trusting its payload,\n70|// which is the Section 7.A v2/v3 major-mismatch outcome (a v2/v3\n71|// mismatch follows the Section 15.1 close/termination rule; the caller\n72|// never trusts a different major's error), and any other unusable\n73|// frame as provider_protocol_error. A v3 plugin is therefore refused\n74|// loudly rather than misread as v2. The failure-error version is still\n75|// selected by the observed major, never the document: major 2 decodes\n76|// under Structured Error 1.0.0 and major 3 would decode under 1.3.0\n77|// (axerror.BindingFor), but no 3.x envelope reaches the decoder while\n78|// this host writes v2 requests. Sending v3 requests, carrying v3\n79|// descriptors in launch/resume bodies, and decoding v3 failures under\n80|// Error 1.3.0 is the dual-stack host follow-up described in this\n81|// task's outcome; until it exists, the 1.3.0 binding is named here\n82|// and implemented in axerror, not silently defaulted to 1.0.0.\n83|package provhost","final_result":true}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c55f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae2b-e184-7c10-8d9d-bac4c231b352"},"sequence":266,"recorded_at":1780531400000527,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0ae2c-8a46-7641-b741-f8df457129f5"},"task_id":"01a0ae2c-8a46-7641-b741-f8df457129f5","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a0ae2c-8a46-7641-b741-f8df457129f5"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c561","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae2b-e184-7c10-8d9d-bac4c231b352"},"sequence":267,"recorded_at":1780531400000529,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0ae2c-89fb-7ea2-9524-9c0752bd77dc"},"task_id":"01a0ae2c-89fb-7ea2-9524-9c0752bd77dc","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0ae2c-89fb-7ea2-9524-9c0752bd77dc","chunk":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(625 line(s) above)\n626|### 2.4 Execution profiles\n627|\n628|The persisted profile enum is <code>standard</code> or <code>yolo</code>.\n629|<code>yolo</code> means the provider-specific unrestricted/no-approval mode; it\n630|does not mean that <code>ax</code> ignores its own ownership, conflict,\n631|allowlist, or integrity checks.\n632|\n633|The immutable Session Record stores the creation profile. Every\n634|<code>provider.launched</code>, <code>task_board.launched</code>,\n635|<code>session.resumed</code>, and <code>fork.created</code> event repeats the\n636|effective ax profile and the digest of the authoritative\n637|<code>profile.changed</code> event from which it was derived, or null when the\n638|Session Record is still the authority. A profile change requires\n639|<code>ax session set-profile NAME PROFILE</code>, a new event under the current\n640|lease, and operator confirmation when changing to <code>yolo</code>. A resume\n641|MUST fail with <code>profile_mapping_unavailable</code> if the adapter cannot\n642|map the stored profile for the probed provider version.\n643|\n644|The effective persisted profile is the Session Record value followed by the\n645|newest authoritative <code>profile.changed</code> event in lease/sequence order.\n646|Losing-lease or ambiguous events MUST NOT change it. Every command that says\n647|“persisted profile” refers to this derived value. The profile source is null\n648|when no authoritative change exists and otherwise is exactly that newest\n649|event's <code>event_id</code>. A Checkpoint's event-head closure fixes both\n650|values for that checkpoint; a bundle, resume, or fork MUST NOT fall back to the\n651|Session Record creation value when the closure contains a later change.\n652|\n653|Fork creates a new authority boundary. Its new Session Record stores the\n654|source-checkpoint effective profile as its creation profile, so the new\n655|session's <code>profile_source_event_id</code> is null. The\n656|<code>fork.created.source_profile_event_id</code> separately preserves the\n657|nullable source-session event used for that projection; it is provenance and\n658|MUST NOT be treated as a profile event in the new session's event chain.\n659|\n660|Provider mapping is evidence, not profile authority. In particular, Pi 0.73.1\n661|maps both ax profiles to <code>default_unrestricted_tool_set</code>; the two ax\n662|profiles remain distinct because the Session/Event authority and confirmation\n663|history remain distinct even when the provider mapping strings are equal.\n664|\n665|The following end-to-end profile fixtures are normative. Let P0 be the Session\n666|Record profile, E1 an authoritative <code>profile.changed</code> event to P1,\n667|and C1 a checkpoint whose event-head closure contains E1:\n668|\n669|| Fixture | Path | Required profile authority |\n670|| --- | --- | --- |\n671|| <code>PROFILE-DIRECT-TAKEOVER</code> | Direct start P0 → E1 → C1 → graceful/force takeover | Bundle is absent; materialization finalize, plugin resume, and resumed event all carry P1/E1 |\n672|| <code>PROFILE-DIRECT-RESUME</code> | Direct stop at C1 → owner resume | Plugin resume and resumed event carry P1/E1; creation P0 is ignored |\n673|| <code>PROFILE-DIRECT-FORK</code> | Fork C1 | New Session Record creation profile is P1; fork event carries P1/null as new-session authority plus <code>source_profile_event_id=E1</code>; resumed carries P1/null |\n674|| <code>PROFILE-TB-TAKEOVER</code> | Task-board start P0 → E1 → export C1 → takeover | Bundle projection carries P1/E1; journaled bridge resume and events use the same pair |\n675|| <code>PROFILE-TB-RESUME</code> | Task-board owner resume from C1 | Bundle, bridge resume, and events agree on P1/E1 |\n676|| <code>PROFILE-TB-FORK</code> | Task-board fork C1 | Source bundle carries P1/E1; new Session Record and bridge resume use P1/null; fork event retains E1 only in <code>source_profile_event_id</code> |\n677|| <code>PROFILE-PI-EQUAL-MAPPING</code> | Pi changes <code>standard</code> to <code>yolo</code> at E1 while both map to <code>default_unrestricted_tool_set</code> | Ax still persists and reports P1/E1; equal provider argv text is not authority to erase E1 |\n678|\n679|Any fixture that uses P0 after C1, omits E1 where the event schema requires its\n680|source, or accepts a bundle profile inconsistent with C1 is\n681|<code>integrity_failure</code> before activation.\n682|\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c563","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae2b-e184-7c10-8d9d-bac4c231b352"},"sequence":268,"recorded_at":1780531400000531,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0ae2c-89fb-7ea2-9524-9c0752bd77dc"},"task_id":"01a0ae2c-89fb-7ea2-9524-9c0752bd77dc","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a0ae2c-89fb-7ea2-9524-9c0752bd77dc"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c565","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae2b-e184-7c10-8d9d-bac4c231b352"},"sequence":269,"recorded_at":1780531400000533,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2"},"call_id":"call_01a0ae2c853f76ca97080f40ebec881d","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(625 line(s) above)\n626|### 2.4 Execution profiles\n627|\n628|The persisted profile enum is <code>standard</code> or <code>yolo</code>.\n629|<code>yolo</code> means the provider-specific unrestricted/no-approval mode; it\n630|does not mean that <code>ax</code> ignores its own ownership, conflict,\n631|allowlist, or integrity checks.\n632|\n633|The immutable Session Record stores the creation profile. Every\n634|<code>provider.launched</code>, <code>task_board.launched</code>,\n635|<code>session.resumed</code>, and <code>fork.created</code> event repeats the\n636|effective ax profile and the digest of the authoritative\n637|<code>profile.changed</code> event from which it was derived, or null when the\n638|Session Record is still the authority. A profile change requires\n639|<code>ax session set-profile NAME PROFILE</code>, a new event under the current\n640|lease, and operator confirmation when changing to <code>yolo</code>. A resume\n641|MUST fail with <code>profile_mapping_unavailable</code> if the adapter cannot\n642|map the stored profile for the probed provider version.\n643|\n644|The effective persisted profile is the Session Record value followed by the\n645|newest authoritative <code>profile.changed</code> event in lease/sequence order.\n646|Losing-lease or ambiguous events MUST NOT change it. Every command that says\n647|“persisted profile” refers to this derived value. The profile source is null\n648|when no authoritative change exists and otherwise is exactly that newest\n649|event's <code>event_id</code>. A Checkpoint's event-head closure fixes both\n650|values for that checkpoint; a bundle, resume, or fork MUST NOT fall back to the\n651|Session Record creation value when the closure contains a later change.\n652|\n653|Fork creates a new authority boundary. Its new Session Record stores the\n654|source-checkpoint effective profile as its creation profile, so the new\n655|session's <code>profile_source_event_id</code> is null. The\n656|<code>fork.created.source_profile_event_id</code> separately preserves the\n657|nullable source-session event used for that projection; it is provenance and\n658|MUST NOT be treated as a profile event in the new session's event chain.\n659|\n660|Provider mapping is evidence, not profile authority. In particular, Pi 0.73.1\n661|maps both ax profiles to <code>default_unrestricted_tool_set</code>; the two ax\n662|profiles remain distinct because the Session/Event authority and confirmation\n663|history remain distinct even when the provider mapping strings are equal.\n664|\n665|The following end-to-end profile fixtures are normative. Let P0 be the Session\n666|Record profile, E1 an authoritative <code>profile.changed</code> event to P1,\n667|and C1 a checkpoint whose event-head closure contains E1:\n668|\n669|| Fixture | Path | Required profile authority |\n670|| --- | --- | --- |\n671|| <code>PROFILE-DIRECT-TAKEOVER</code> | Direct start P0 → E1 → C1 → graceful/force takeover | Bundle is absent; materialization finalize, plugin resume, and resumed event all carry P1/E1 |\n672|| <code>PROFILE-DIRECT-RESUME</code> | Direct stop at C1 → owner resume | Plugin resume and resumed event carry P1/E1; creation P0 is ignored |\n673|| <code>PROFILE-DIRECT-FORK</code> | Fork C1 | New Session Record creation profile is P1; fork event carries P1/null as new-session authority plus <code>source_profile_event_id=E1</code>; resumed carries P1/null |\n674|| <code>PROFILE-TB-TAKEOVER</code> | Task-board start P0 → E1 → export C1 → takeover | Bundle projection carries P1/E1; journaled bridge resume and events use the same pair |\n675|| <code>PROFILE-TB-RESUME</code> | Task-board owner resume from C1 | Bundle, bridge resume, and events agree on P1/E1 |\n676|| <code>PROFILE-TB-FORK</code> | Task-board fork C1 | Source bundle carries P1/E1; new Session Record and bridge resume use P1/null; fork event retains E1 only in <code>source_profile_event_id</code> |\n677|| <code>PROFILE-PI-EQUAL-MAPPING</code> | Pi changes <code>standard</code> to <code>yolo</code> at E1 while both map to <code>default_unrestricted_tool_set</code> | Ax still persists and reports P1/E1; equal provider argv text is not authority to erase E1 |\n678|\n679|Any fixture that uses P0 after C1, omits E1 where the event schema requires its\n680|source, or accepts a bundle profile inconsistent with C1 is\n681|<code>integrity_failure</code> before activation.\n682|\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c567","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae2b-e184-7c10-8d9d-bac4c231b352"},"sequence":270,"recorded_at":1780531400000535,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2"},"call_id":"call_01a0ae2c877c75b2935b16c7cda267a8","text":"Read text file `internal/provhost/doc.go`.\n1|// Package provhost implements the ax host side of the provider plugin\n2|// JSON-over-stdio protocol of pinned specification Section 7.2: one-frame\n3|// JSONL transport, deadlines, size limits, stdout/stderr separation,\n4|// operation dispatch, and status recovery.\n5|//\n6|// The actor every Section 7.2 RFC 2119 keyword governs here is the ax host\n7|// (this repository). The provider plugin is a child process this package\n8|// starts but never trusts: every byte it emits is attacker-influenced, so\n9|// every frame is validated before any member is used, and a different-major\n10|// payload is never parsed far enough to trust its error code, retryable\n11|// bit, details, or authority fields (Section 15.1).\n12|//\n13|// This package owns the envelope layer and the operation-layer\n14|// conformance decoders. Operation dispatch means the host maps an\n15|// operation name to one plugin invocation and refuses unknown names\n16|// locally without spawning; the conformance decoders validate what a\n17|// plugin returned: the closed manifest (7.3), probe and capability\n18|// gate (7.4, 8.3), profile mapping (7.7), quiescence proof (7.6),\n19|// SpawnPlan success bodies for launch and resume, the Provider\n20|// Identity Record and identify-session result (5.5), and the single\n21|// (operation, operation_id) mutation key (7.5). Full Section 7.5\n22|// request-body vocabularies beyond these surfaces, and the\n23|// materialize commit/rollback result bodies, cross this package\n24|// opaquely: a body must still be a JSON object, and the\n25|// materialize-status body this package interprets for recovery must\n26|// satisfy the state/nullability/identity rules Section 7.5 states\n27|// for ProviderTransactionStatus.\n28|//\n29|// A failed read is never an absence: a crashed, timed-out, oversized,\n30|// unparsable, or correlation-mismatched invocation fails with a local\n31|// Structured Error 1.0.0, never with a partial response the caller could\n32|// mistake for a result. Status recovery is fail-closed the same way: an\n33|// unknown or integrity-invalid transaction fails with integrity_failure\n34|// and must be quarantined rather than represented as a successful status.\n35|//\n36|// The package mutates no durable state of its own and keeps no cross-call\n37|// cache: Host starts one plugin process per operation, so successive calls\n38|// are separate processes observing durable state through the authority the\n39|// caller passes, which is the property the cross-process recovery cases\n40|// rest on. Retry decisions belong to the caller; every local failure this\n41|// package emits is non-retryable, because nothing observed at an unusable\n42|// frame licenses a safe-retry claim.\n43|//\n44|// Stated bounds: stderr capture is capped at 1 MiB (an implementation\n45|// resource bound, not a protocol limit); error details carry the failure\n46|// class, member names, and the observed version string only — never\n47|// environment or stderr content. Mutation receipts live in the\n48|// provider transaction document and the plugin, not in the host: the\n49|// host holds no cross-call cache, so cross-crash durability of\n50|// (operation, operation_id) results is the document's property, and\n51|// the host side pins the sole key shape, byte-identical retry bytes,\n52|// and the mismatch surface. The Section 8 provider/platform cell\n53|// values are evidence labels, not host behavior: the host pins the\n54|// fail-closed direction (conditional, unsupported, and unknown are\n55|// never usable) for every capability-by-status tuple rather than one\n56|// cell value, resolves the Section 8.2 documented store roots for\n57|// discovery binding, and refuses the Section 8.4 disabled resume\n58|// tuples. Section 8.2 store exclusions and the remaining Section 7.5\n59|// request vocabularies have no host production yet.\n60|//\n61|// Provider Protocol 3.0.0 (§7.A), which the v0.5.0 catalog pins\n62|// alongside 2.0.0, is owned by internal/terminalbackend, not by this\n63|// package: the v3-specific production rule — parsing the closed v3\n64|// TerminalDescriptor and matching it against the AX-validated\n65|// host-local binding before a provider process is launched or observed\n66|// — is terminalbackend.AdmitProviderDescriptor, and the traceability\n67|// registry records section 7.A against terminalbackend for it. This\n68|// host speaks major 2 only: it classifies any recognizable 3.x\n69|// envelope as incompatible_protocol without trusting its payload,\n70|// which is the Section 7.A v2/v3 major-mismatch outcome (a v2/v3\n71|// mismatch follows the Section 15.1 close/termination rule; the caller\n72|// never trusts a different major's error), and any other unusable\n73|// frame as provider_protocol_error. A v3 plugin is therefore refused\n74|// loudly rather than misread as v2. The failure-error version is still\n75|// selected by the observed major, never the document: major 2 decodes\n76|// under Structured Error 1.0.0 and major 3 would decode under 1.3.0\n77|// (axerror.BindingFor), but no 3.x envelope reaches the decoder while\n78|// this host writes v2 requests. Sending v3 requests, carrying v3\n79|// descriptors in launch/resume bodies, and decoding v3 failures under\n80|// Error 1.3.0 is the dual-stack host follow-up described in this\n81|// task's outcome; until it exists, the 1.3.0 binding is named here\n82|// and implemented in axerror, not silently defaulted to 1.0.0.\n83|package provhost","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
