{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5f5","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae2b-e184-7c10-8d9d-bac4c231b352"},"sequence":341,"recorded_at":1780531400000677,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"be9157de-ded3-4e9d-94df-e7cb793fb0f2"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0ae2c-b869-7233-8329-8614c4645096"},"task_id":"01a0ae2c-b869-7233-8329-8614c4645096","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0ae2c-b869-7233-8329-8614c4645096","chunk":"Read text file `internal/provhost/profile_resolve.go`.\n1|package provhost\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/secprim\"\n5|)\n6|\n7|// This file resolves Section 7.7 execution-profile mappings against the\n8|// exact probed provider build and projects the sanitized launch argv a\n9|// launch or resume operation executes. The table in profile.go is the\n10|// single source of the required yolo flags; this file decides when\n11|// those flags may be applied.\n12|//\n13|// Resolution requires the exact-version probe result as a BuildTuple:\n14|// the tuple passes the Section 8.4 resume gate (CheckResumeTuple, so\n15|// an unknown provider, platform, or architecture, a direct Qwen\n16|// claim, and an unverified Muse build refuse before any mapping is\n17|// considered) and names the resolved provider exactly, because a\n18|// mapping decided for one build must never silently apply to\n19|// another. A provider without a Section 7.7 row — Qwen direct,\n20|// future plugins — and a Pi build other than the table's pinned\n21|// 0.73.1 cannot be mapped for the probed version and refuse with\n22|// the Section 2.4 profile_mapping_unavailable resume failure.\n23|//\n24|// The launch projection emits the unrestricted flag only for yolo\n25|// and only the exact table token: an absent flag (nothing to emit\n26|// where yolo requires one is impossible by construction, so absence\n27|// is the caller's empty base, refused structurally) or a changed\n28|// flag (any table token already present in the base, or any\n29|// foreign table token) fails closed. Standard omits every\n30|// unrestricted flag and refuses a base that already carries one —\n31|// the decidable half of the machine-local-alias rule: an exact\n32|// token match over the six table flags plus the documented codex\n33|// --yolo alias. A locally aliased shell word that expands to\n34|// unrestricted mode without spelling a table token is beyond what\n35|// argv inspection can decide (shell aliases never expand under\n36|// execve argv, which is the structural defense, owned by secprim);\n37|// that residual is stated, not silently admitted.\n38|//\n39|// The Section 5.1 argv bounds enforced here (1..128 elements, each\n40|// 1..4,096 bytes, 65,536 bytes total) are the host-side twin of\n41|// the SpawnPlan wire bounds checkSpawnArgv enforces: the two never\n42|// diverge, pinned by the agreement test that drives identical\n43|// vectors at each limit through both entries. Structural argv\n44|// validity (non-empty, NUL-free, UTF-8) stays with secprim and is\n45|// driven, not duplicated.\n46|\n47|// codexAliasYOLO is the codex --yolo alias Section 7.7 documents as\n48|// accepted by the current CLI. The required adapter mapping stays\n49|// the long form in profileYOLOMapping; the alias exists here only\n50|// so the launch projection recognizes (and refuses) it as an\n51|// unrestricted token rather than passing it through as an\n52|// innocuous word.\n53|const codexAliasYOLO = \"--yolo\"\n54|\n55|// piPinnedVersion is the exact Pi build the Section 7.7 table maps.\n56|// Every other Pi version refuses profile_mapping_unavailable:\n57|// the table pins 0.73.1, and no other row covers Pi.\n58|const piPinnedVersion = \"0.73.1\"\n59|\n60|// ResolvedMapping is one Section 7.7 mapping decision for an exact\n61|// probed build: the provider and profile it was resolved for, the\n62|// probed version it is bound to, the SpawnPlan profile_mapping\n63|// value (the exact table flag for yolo, empty for standard, which\n64|// omits every unrestricted flag), and whether both profiles share\n65|// the provider's default full tool set (Pi only — probe and launch\n66|// output MUST disclose that equivalence).\n67|type ResolvedMapping struct {\n68|\tProviderID string\n69|\tProfile    string\n70|\tVersion    string\n71|\tMapping    string\n72|\tEquivalent bool\n73|}\n74|\n75|// ResolveMapping resolves the Section 7.7 adapter mapping for one\n76|// provider under one profile against the exact probed build tuple.\n77|// The tuple passes the Section 8.4 resume gate and must name the\n78|// resolved provider; the profile must be inside the vocabulary; the\n79|// provider must carry a Section 7.7 row and Pi must be the pinned\n80|// 0.73.1 build. Unmappable combinations refuse with the Section\n81|// 2.4 profile_mapping_unavailable failure naming the provider,\n82|// probed version, and profile; miscorrelated inputs refuse as\n83|// caller errors.\n84|func ResolveMapping(providerID, profile string, tuple BuildTuple) (ResolvedMapping, error) {\n85|\tempty := ResolvedMapping{}\n86|\t// The caller's provider argument is classified before the\n87|\t// tuple gate runs: garbage fails as a caller error, while a\n88|\t// well-formed provider without a Section 7.7 row (Qwen\n89|\t// direct, future plugins) is the mapping gap Section 2.4\n90|\t// names. Running the tuple gate first would misclassify\n91|\t// both as tuple refusals.\n92|\tif !validProviderID(providerID) {\n93|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"mapping provider is not a provider id\")\n94|\t\tif err != nil {\n95|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n96|\t\t}\n97|\t\treturn empty, failure\n98|\t}\n99|\tif _, ok := profileYOLOMapping[providerID]; !ok {\n100|\t\tfailure, err := failMappingUnavailable(\"mapping has no row for this provider\", providerID, tuple.ProviderVersion, profile)\n101|\t\tif err != nil {\n102|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n103|\t\t}\n104|\t\treturn empty, failure\n105|\t}\n106|\tif err := CheckResumeTuple(tuple); err != nil {\n107|\t\treturn empty, err\n108|\t}\n109|\tif tuple.ProviderID != providerID {\n110|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"mapping tuple names another provider\")\n111|\t\tif err != nil {\n112|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n113|\t\t}\n114|\t\treturn empty, failure\n115|\t}\n116|\tif !profileNames[profile] {\n117|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"mapping profile is not standard or yolo\")\n118|\t\tif err != nil {\n119|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n120|\t\t}\n121|\t\treturn empty, failure\n122|\t}\n123|\tif providerID == \"pi\" && tuple.ProviderVersion != piPinnedVersion {\n124|\t\tfailure, err := failMappingUnavailable(\"mapping pins pi to probed 0.73.1\", providerID, tuple.ProviderVersion, profile)\n125|\t\tif err != nil {\n126|\t\t\treturn empty, err\n127|\t\t}\n128|\t\treturn empty, failure\n129|\t}\n130|\t// The flag value comes from the single table source. Both\n131|\t// refusals below it are unreachable on this path — the row and\n132|\t// the profile were just established — but the value must not be\n133|\t// retyped here.\n134|\tmapping, err := ProfileMapping(providerID, profile)\n135|\tif err != nil {\n136|\t\treturn empty, err\n137|\t}\n138|\treturn ResolvedMapping{\n139|\t\tProviderID: providerID,\n140|\t\tProfile:    profile,\n141|\t\tVersion:    tuple.ProviderVersion,\n142|\t\tMapping:    mapping,\n143|\t\tEquivalent: providerID == \"pi\",\n144|\t}, nil\n145|}\n146|\n147|// ProjectLaunchArgv projects the sanitized launch argv for one\n148|// resolved mapping over the caller base (the provider binary with\n149|// its subcommand). The resolution must name this provider and\n150|// profile, and the base must be structurally valid. Yolo appends\n151|// exactly the table flag once — Pi appends nothing because its\n152|// mapping is report-only — and standard returns the base\n153|// unchanged. Any unrestricted token already present in the base\n154|// refuses: the exact required flag is a duplicate, any other\n155|// table token or the codex alias is a changed flag under yolo and\n156|// an alias reuse under standard. The Section 5.1 argv bounds apply\n157|// to the projected vector.\n158|func ProjectLaunchArgv(base []string, providerID, profile string, resolved ResolvedMapping) ([]string, error) {\n159|\tif resolved.ProviderID != providerID || resolved.Profile != profile {\n160|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"launch mapping does not match the provider profile\")\n161|\t\tif err != nil {\n162|\t\t\treturn nil, err\n163|\t\t}\n164|\t\treturn nil, failure\n165|\t}\n166|\tif err := secprim.CheckArgv(base); err != nil {\n167|\t\treturn nil, err\n168|\t}\n169|\ttokens := unrestrictedTokens()\n170|\tfor _, element := range base {\n171|\t\tif tokens[element] {\n172|\t\t\t// The duplicate arm applies only where the mapping\n173|\t\t\t// is an argv flag: Pi's report-only mapping is\n174|\t\t\t// never appended, so its presence in a base is a\n175|\t\t\t// changed flag like any other foreign token.\n176|\t\t\tif profile == ProfileYOLO && providerID != \"pi\" && element == resolved.Mapping && resolved.Mapping != \"\" {\n177|\t\t\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"launch argv already carries the profile flag\")\n178|\t\t\t\tif err != nil {\n179|\t\t\t\t\treturn nil, err\n180|\t\t\t\t}\n181|\t\t\t\treturn nil, failure\n182|\t\t\t}\n183|\t\t\tif profile == ProfileYOLO {\n184|\t\t\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"launch argv carries a changed unrestricted flag\")\n185|\t\t\t\tif err != nil {\n186|\t\t\t\t\treturn nil, err\n187|\t\t\t\t}\n188|\t\t\t\treturn nil, failure\n189|\t\t\t}\n190|\t\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"launch argv reuses an unrestricted flag under standard\")\n191|\t\t\tif err != nil {\n192|\t\t\t\treturn nil, err\n193|\t\t\t}\n194|\t\t\treturn nil, failure\n195|\t\t}\n196|\t}\n197|\tprojected := append([]string(nil), base...)\n198|\t// Pi's mapping is report-only: it is never an argv token, so\n199|\t// yolo appends nothing for Pi and the disclosure travels in\n200|\t// the resolution, not the command line.\n201|\tif profile == ProfileYOLO && providerID != \"pi\" && resolved.Mapping != \"\" {\n202|\t\tprojected = append(projected, resolved.Mapping)\n203|\t}\n204|\tif err := checkProjectedArgvBounds(projected); err != nil {\n205|\t\treturn nil, err\n206|\t}\n207|\treturn projected, nil\n208|}\n209|\n210|// unrestrictedTokens is the exact token set the launch projection\n211|// refuses in a caller base: every Section 7.7 yolo flag plus the\n212|// documented codex alias. It derives from the table, never retypes\n213|// it: a new table row is refused in bases from the same change.\n214|func unrestrictedTokens() map[string]bool {\n215|\ttokens := make(map[string]bool, len(profileYOLOMapping)+1)\n216|\tfor _, flag := range profileYOLOMapping {\n217|\t\ttokens[flag] = true\n218|\t}\n219|\ttokens[codexAliasYOLO] = true\n220|\treturn tokens\n221|}\n222|\n223|// checkProjectedArgvBounds enforces the Section 5.1 numeric argv\n224|// bounds on the projected vector: at most 128 elements, each at\n225|// most 4,096 bytes, 65,536 bytes total. Byte length is the\n226|// exec-bound quantity, so elements count bytes here. This is the\n227|// host-side twin of the wire bounds checkSpawnArgv enforces; the\n228|// agreement test drives identical vectors at each limit through\n229|// both entries. Structural validity (non-empty, NUL-free, UTF-8)\n230|// stays with secprim.CheckArgv, which the projection drives\n231|// before appending its known-good constant.\n232|func checkProjectedArgvBounds(projected []string) error {\n233|\tif len(projected) > 128 {\n234|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"launch argv exceeds 128 elements\")\n235|\t\tif err != nil {\n236|\t\t\treturn err\n237|\t\t}\n238|\t\treturn failure\n239|\t}\n240|\ttotal := 0\n241|\tfor _, element := range projected {\n242|\t\tif len(element) > 4096 {\n243|\t\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"launch argv element exceeds 4096 bytes\")\n244|\t\t\tif err != nil {\n245|\t\t\t\treturn err\n246|\t\t\t}\n247|\t\t\treturn failure\n248|\t\t}\n249|\t\ttotal += len(element)\n250|\t}\n251|\tif total > maxSpawnArgvBytes {\n252|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"launch argv exceeds 65536 bytes total\")\n253|\t\tif err != nil {\n254|\t\t\treturn err\n255|\t\t}\n256|\t\treturn failure\n257|\t}\n258|\treturn nil\n259|}","final_result":true}}}
